{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>One CSS property, and the scrollbar started jumping around</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A long documentation page — a dozen sections, a lot of DOM — got one line added: <code>content-visibility: auto</code> on each section. Initial render got dramatically faster, exactly as promised. Then someone scrolled down and the scrollbar thumb visibly jumped, the page height kept snapping around, and it looked worse than before the "optimization."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>content-visibility: auto</code> does exactly what it says: sections not currently relevant to the user get skipped — no layout, no paint — until they're needed. What it doesn't do on its own is reserve any space for what it's skipping, so a skipped section collapses toward zero height until the browser actually renders it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a containment trade-off, not a bug: <code>content-visibility: auto</code> applies size containment as part of skipping render work, and size containment means the element's size is computed without looking at its contents at all. Skip the render, and by definition you've also skipped knowing how tall the thing is.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Tell the browser how tall the thing is before it skips rendering it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The fix is a second property, <code>contain-intrinsic-size</code>, set alongside <code>content-visibility: auto</code> on the same element. It gives the browser a placeholder size to use while a section's real content is skipped, so the page's total height stays roughly correct the whole time, not just after everything's been scrolled past once.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Use the <code>auto</code> keyword form: <code>contain-intrinsic-size: auto 300px</code>. The <code>300px</code> is the fallback guess for a section that's never been rendered yet; the <code>auto</code> part tells the browser to remember the section's real measured height the first time it does render, and reuse that remembered size the next time it's skipped, instead of falling back to the guess again.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fallback size doesn't need to be exact, it needs to be close enough that the layout shift from placeholder-to-real-content stays under whatever your CLS budget tolerates. A skeleton-loader-style estimate per section type (short section vs. long section) beats one constant applied uniformly across sections of very different real heights.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The changelog page that "optimized" itself into visible jank</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A product's changelog page had grown to hundreds of entries and felt sluggish on load. Someone added <code>content-visibility: auto</code> to each entry's wrapper, saw the initial render time drop sharply in a Lighthouse run, and shipped it. QA caught the regression: scrolling down the page now visibly stuttered, and the scrollbar thumb resized itself repeatedly as entries came into view.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Every unrendered entry was collapsing to just its padding, a few pixels, instead of its real height, somewhere around 150-300px depending on the entry. The page's total scrollable height was wrong until every entry had been scrolled past at least once, so the browser kept having to recompute it mid-scroll, which is exactly what a user sees as jank.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fix added <code>contain-intrinsic-size: auto 220px</code> (roughly the median entry height, measured from real data) to the same rule. First render stayed just as fast, since skipping is still happening, but the placeholder height meant the total page height was correct from the start, and each entry's <code>auto</code>-remembered size self-corrected after its first real render, so the initial 220px guess only mattered once per entry.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

