{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>content-visibility has real limits and one common accessibility trap</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Don't apply <code>content-visibility: auto</code> to the whole page in one wrapper — it needs to be on individual sections so the browser can skip some while rendering others. One giant wrapper just moves the same all-or-nothing rendering cost around instead of actually reducing it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Find-in-page (Ctrl/Cmd+F) and fragment-link navigation (<code>#section-id</code>) both work correctly through skipped content — the browser temporarily renders a matched section even if it's currently skipped. This is a deliberate spec guarantee, not something you need to work around.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Watch for interaction with <code>position: sticky</code> or <code>fixed</code> elements nested inside a <code>content-visibility: auto</code> ancestor — size containment can affect how their containing block resolves, producing sticky headers that stop sticking correctly once their ancestor starts skipping render. Test that combination explicitly rather than assuming it's unaffected.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

